🧩 1. Use a Subquery when it’s simple and used once

A subquery is best when:

The logic is short and straightforward
You only need it once
It fits naturally inside a WHERE, SELECT, or FROM
✅ Example: Filter based on an aggregate
SELECT name, salary
FROM employees
WHERE salary > (
    SELECT AVG(salary)
    FROM employees
);


🧩 2. Use a CTE when the logic is complex or reused

A CTE (Common Table Expression) is better when:

You need to reuse the result
The query is getting hard to read
You’re breaking logic into steps
You’re doing recursive queries (CTEs are required for this)
✅ Example: Reuse a computed dataset
WITH avg_salary AS (
    SELECT AVG(salary) AS avg_sal
    FROM employees
)
SELECT name, salary
FROM employees, avg_salary
WHERE employees.salary > avg_salary.avg_sal

🧩 3. When you need multiple steps → CTE wins
❌ Subquery version (harder to read)
SELECT department, COUNT(*)
FROM (
    SELECT *
    FROM employees
    WHERE salary > (
        SELECT AVG(salary) FROM employees
    )
) high_paid
GROUP BY department;
✅ CTE version (cleaner)
WITH high_paid AS (
    SELECT *
    FROM employees
    WHERE salary > (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM employees)
)
SELECT department, COUNT(*)
FROM high_paid
GROUP BY department;
